Create flags:
      --metro=<metro>
          Metro to deploy in. Defaults to the profile's default metro.
          [examples: fra, sfo]
  -n, --name=<name>
          Instance name: a lowercase DNS label of up to 118 characters, unique per metro. Generated from the image name when omitted.
      --tag=<tag> ...
          Tags for grouping and filtering: up to 16, each 1 to 256 characters of letters, digits and -+_.:=. Not visible to the guest.
          [example: env-prod]
      --annotation=<key>=<value> ...
          Key-value metadata: up to 256 entries with Kubernetes-style [prefix/]name keys. Unlike tags, annotations reach the guest in its start data.
          [examples: env=production, example.com/team=platform]
      --image=<name>:<tag>
          Image to deploy, as an OCI reference or a full registry URL. Required unless template, branch or checkpoint is given, and cannot be combined with them.
          [examples: nginx:latest, my-app:v1.2.3]
      --pull-policy=<policy>
          When to pull the image relative to the node's cache: always, if_not_present or never.
          [examples: always, if_not_present, never]
      --type=<type>
          Virtual machine type.
            micro: Firecracker microVM (default)
            full: QEMU virtual machine with GPU support, needs a plan with full VM support and has no scale-to-zero, templates, branching or checkpoints.
          [examples: micro, full]
      --args=<arg>
          Command-line arguments passed to the instance at start. Up to 128.
          [example: --port=8080]
  -e, --env=<key>=<value> ...
          Environment variable as KEY=value. Up to 256.
          [example: DEBUG=true]
  -m, --memory=<size>
          Memory for the instance. Defaults to 128MiB, within the range the plan allows (see quotas.limits.memory on the metro).
          [examples: 128MiB, 1GiB]
      --vcpus=<n>
          Number of vCPUs. Defaults to 1, within the range the plan allows (see quotas.limits.vcpus on the metro).
          [examples: 1, 2, 4]
      --gpus=<n>
          Number of GPUs to attach, 0 or 1. Requires type full and a plan with GPU support. The GPU stays assigned until the instance is deleted. Cannot be combined with template, branch or checkpoint.
          [examples: 0, 1]
      --service=<name>
          Service group to join, by name or UUID. Leave unset and give service.services or service.domains to create a group owned by the instance. Without any service the instance is reachable only privately.
  -p, --publish=<src>:<dest>[/<handlers>] ...
          Published port as SOURCE:DESTINATION[/HANDLERS], with handlers joined by +. Port 80 must use http, port 443 http+tls, and any other port tls only.
            http: load balance per HTTP request instead of per TCP connection
            tls: terminate TLS at the platform
            redirect: redirect HTTP on port 80 to HTTPS, as in 80:443/http+redirect.
          [examples: 443:8080/http+tls, 80:443/http+redirect]
      --domain=<fqdn> ...
          Domain for the group. Up to 8 per group.
            myapp: a bare label becomes a subdomain of the metro
            example.com: a dotted name is a custom domain, with a certificate issued automatically unless given as name=example.com,certificate=my-cert.
          [examples: example.com, myapp]
  -v, --volume=[<name>]:<path>[:<options>] ...
          Volume to attach as NAME:/MOUNT[:ro], where the mount path must be absolute. On create, add :size=SIZE to make a new volume with the instance, and leave NAME empty to have one generated. Up to 4 per instance.
          [examples: my-vol:/data, cache:/tmp:ro, :/data:size=1GiB]
      --rom=name=<name>,image=<ref>,at=<path> ...
          Read-only ROM device from an image or a local directory. The name uses letters, digits, - and _, and appears as /dev/ukp_rom_NAME in the guest. Up to 8 per instance.
          [examples: name=my-rom,image=myuser/my-rom:latest,at=/rom0, name=mydata,dir=./mydata,at=/rom]
      --plugin=name=<name>,image=<ref>[,config=<json>] ...
          Plugin loaded from an image, with an optional JSON config passed to it on start. Up to 8 per instance.
          [examples: name=sandbox,image=plugins/sandbox:latest, name=sandbox,image=plugins/sandbox:latest,config={"persist_path":"/data"}]
      --scale-to-zero=<key>=<value>
          Scale-to-zero options. Requires a service group.
            policy: on | idle (also while open TCP connections are idle, needs stateful=true) | off
            cooldown-time: ms of inactivity before scaling to zero (default 1000, minimum 100)
            notify-time: ms the instance is warned beforehand, 0 disables and the value must be below cooldown-time
            stateful: true keeps RAM in a snapshot and resumes from it, false restarts from scratch.
          [examples: on, policy=on,cooldown-time=300, policy=on,stateful=true,cooldown-time=500,notify-time=100]
      --autokill=<key>=<value>
          Autokill options.
            time: time after the instance stops before it is deleted, such as 5s or 24h, 0 disables
            num-requests: delete the instance after it has served this many requests or connections.
          [examples: time=5s, num-requests=100, time=5s,num-requests=100]
      --restart=<policy>
          Restart policy. Restarts back off from immediate up to 5m. Cannot be combined with the delete-on-stop feature.
            never: never restart (default)
            always: restart whenever the instance exits or crashes
            on-failure: restart only after a crash.
          [examples: always, on-failure, never]
      --sched-priority=<priority>
          Scheduling priority: normal (default), medium, high or admin. Requires the override_vm_priority permission.
          [examples: normal, medium, high, admin]
      --autostart
          Start the instance right after creation. When false it stays stopped, except that scale-to-zero puts it in standby, where incoming traffic starts it.
      --replicas=<n>
          Additional identical instances to create, so replicas=2 creates three in total.
          [examples: 1, 3]
      --feature=<feature> ...
          Instance feature to enable.
            delete-on-stop: delete the instance when it stops, not with a restart policy or delete-lock
            nested-virt: expose virtualization extensions to the guest, needs the nested_virt permission.
          [example: delete-on-stop]
      --template=<name>
          Create from an instance template, by name or UUID. The clone inherits its configuration and, when the template has a snapshot, its memory state. Cannot be combined with image, branch, checkpoint, type or gpus.
      --branch=<instance>
          Clone an existing instance, by name or UUID. A running source is snapshotted asynchronously and keeps running. Cannot be combined with image, args, env, memory, vcpus, type or gpus.
      --checkpoint=<checkpoint>
          Create from a checkpoint, by name or UUID, restoring its memory and disk. Cannot be combined with image, args, env, memory, vcpus, type or gpus.
      --rm
          Automatically delete the instance when it stops.

